WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.236
Many firmware projects use `no_std`, fixed
memory layouts,

2
00:00:03.316 --> 00:00:06.697
static allocation, bounded buffers, or
carefully controlled heap usage.

3
00:00:06.777 --> 00:00:10.586
These constraints shape security because
the device may have limited diagnostics,

4
00:00:10.666 --> 00:00:14.476
strict timing behavior, and little
tolerance for unbounded allocation or surprise

5
00:00:14.556 --> 00:00:15.254
failure paths.

6
00:00:15.334 --> 00:00:19.036
Rust ownership and borrowing help clarify
who owns buffers, peripherals, state,

7
00:00:19.116 --> 00:00:19.706
and handles.

8
00:00:19.786 --> 00:00:23.596
That clarity reduces classes of
memory-safety risk in safe code and gives reviewers

9
00:00:23.676 --> 00:00:26.359
a better way to reason about reuse,
mutation, and lifetime.

10
00:00:26.439 --> 00:00:30.194
In firmware, that also helps explain which
component is responsible for a resource

11
00:00:30.274 --> 00:00:31.240
at each point in time.

12
00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:34.057
Memory-safe code can still fail through
resource pressure.

13
00:00:34.137 --> 00:00:37.303
Stack depth, buffer sizes, queue lengths,
message rates, recursion,

14
00:00:37.383 --> 00:00:41.085
allocation policy, integer conversions,
and worst-case input sizes all matter.

15
00:00:41.165 --> 00:00:44.760
A firmware path can be memory safe and
still become unreliable if it accepts more

16
00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:46.289
work than the device can handle.

17
00:00:46.369 --> 00:00:49.803
Defensive Rust firmware favors bounded
data structures, explicit limits,

18
00:00:49.883 --> 00:00:53.746
predictable failure behavior, and careful
conversion of numeric values into sizes,

19
00:00:53.826 --> 00:00:55.275
indexes, counts, and timeouts.

20
00:00:55.355 --> 00:00:58.842
When a heap is present, the policy for
allocation and failure should be clear.

21
00:00:58.922 --> 00:01:02.464
When static buffers are used, ownership
and maximum use should be documented.

22
00:01:02.544 --> 00:01:05.387
The practical review question is what
happens under pressure.

23
00:01:05.467 --> 00:01:08.311
If input is too large, if a queue is full,
if a task misses timing,

24
00:01:08.391 --> 00:01:10.645
if a buffer is exhausted, or if a
conversion fails,

25
00:01:10.725 --> 00:01:12.764
the device should move to a defined
behavior.

26
00:01:12.844 --> 00:01:14.668
Secure firmware is not only memory safe;

27
00:01:14.748 --> 00:01:17.860
it is bounded enough to operate safely
when conditions are not ideal.
